[Variation "The 1.e4 route against the French"]

{ Volume 1: The King's Indian Attack from First Principles. Why it matters: Starting with 1.e4 invites a French structure while d3 and Nd2 keep White out of the main French center and preserve the KIA plan. } 1. e4 e6 2. d3 d5 { d5: Black claims central space and challenges White to justify the restrained d3 setup. } 3. Nd2 Nf6 4. Ngf3 Be7 { Be7: Black changes a bishop's diagonal; identify the pawn break that will make the diagonal useful. } 5. g3 O-O 6. Bg2 c5 { c5: Black attacks d4 and accelerates queenside expansion. } 7. O-O Nc6 8. Re1 { Re1: White places a rook behind a pawn break or file expected to open. Black plan: Black develops naturally, gains space with ...d5 and ...c5, and attacks the base of White's center before e5 becomes stable. White plan: White secures e4, develops the kingside, and prepares e5 only when the resulting closed center favors a kingside attack. Decision: If White plays e5 now, where must the f3 knight go after ...Nd7, and can Black challenge the pawn chain? Danger: Believing the opening name guarantees an attack even when e5 is tactically premature. Memory rule: The 1.e4 KIA reaches a French shell; e5 must be earned, not scheduled. } *

