[Variation "The 1.Nf3 flexible route"]

{ Volume 1: The King's Indian Attack from First Principles. Why it matters: 1.Nf3 delays the central pawn commitment and can avoid some forcing defenses, but Black may choose an active ...e5 setup of its own. } 1. Nf3 d6 2. g3 e5 { e5: Black builds a symmetrical center that requires White to create a new pawn break. } 3. Bg2 Nf6 4. O-O Be7 { Be7: Black changes a bishop's diagonal; identify the pawn break that will make the diagonal useful. } 5. d3 O-O 6. Nbd2 Nc6 { Nc6: Black reroutes a knight to c6; name the destination job and the useful move conceded to the opponent. } 7. e4 { e4: White builds the KIA center but must account for Black's immediate central break. Black plan: Black occupies the center, develops quickly, and prepares ...Re8 or ...d5 before White creates a space advantage. White plan: White uses the completed fianchetto to decide between a closed e4 center, c4 pressure, or a later d4 expansion. Decision: What center did Black choose, and which white pawn break challenges it without blocking the g2 bishop? Danger: Using flexibility only to postpone every central decision until Black controls all useful breaks. Memory rule: A flexible move order is valuable only if it creates a better choice later. } *

