[Variation "The e5 space wedge"]

{ Volume 2: The Locked e5 Center. Why it matters: e5 gains kingside space, restricts the f6 knight, and closes the center, making a wing attack plausible while fixing d3 as the pawn-chain base. } 1. e4 e6 2. d3 d5 { d5: Black claims central space and challenges White to justify the restrained d3 setup. } 3. Nd2 Nf6 4. Ngf3 Be7 { Be7: Black changes a bishop's diagonal; identify the pawn break that will make the diagonal useful. } 5. g3 O-O 6. Bg2 c5 { c5: Black attacks d4 and accelerates queenside expansion. } 7. O-O Nc6 8. Re1 b5 { b5: Black gains queenside space and begins the race against White's king attack. } 9. e5 Nd7 10. h4 { h4: White creates a possible kingside hook and supports h5. Black plan: Black attacks the chain with ...f6, gains queenside space, and uses the d4 square before White's kingside pieces coordinate. White plan: White keeps e5 supported, reroutes pieces toward the king, and uses h4, Nf1-h2-g4, or Bf4 before opening lines. Decision: Can Black undermine e5 immediately, and which defender or recapture keeps White's pieces active? Danger: Advancing e5 without a route for the f3 knight or enough support for the pawn chain. Memory rule: e5 buys kingside space; White must use the space before Black attacks its base. } *

