{ Volume 2: The Locked e5 Center. Why it matters: The advanced e5 pawn is only as stable as d3 and the pieces behind it; pressure on d3 can make the whole attacking structure brittle. } 1. e4 e6 2. d3 d5 { d5: Black claims central space and challenges White to justify the restrained d3 setup. } 3. Nd2 Nf6 4. Ngf3 Be7 { Be7: Black changes a bishop's diagonal; identify the pawn break that will make the diagonal useful. } 5. g3 O-O 6. Bg2 c5 { c5: Black attacks d4 and accelerates queenside expansion. } 7. O-O Nc6 8. Re1 b5 { b5: Black gains queenside space and begins the race against White's king attack. } 9. e5 Nd7 10. h4 a5 { a5: Black changes the pawn skeleton; reassess the chain, breaks, weak squares, and king exposure. } 11. Nf1 { Nf1: White reroutes a knight to f1; name the destination job and the useful move conceded to the opponent. Black plan: Black combines ...b5-b4, ...a5, and ...Ba6 or ...f6 so White must defend d3 while attacking elsewhere. White plan: White defends d3 economically, clears d2 for pieces, and avoids tying the queen and rooks to a static weakness. Decision: Which black piece can attack d3, and can White meet it with development rather than passive defense? Danger: Concentrating every piece on the kingside while the chain's base falls with tempo. Memory rule: Attack from the head of the chain, but protect the base with the fewest pieces possible. } *

