{ Volume 2: The Locked e5 Center. Why it matters: Because White's pawn stands on d3 rather than d4, Black often receives the d4 outpost for a knight or bishop and can use it to interrupt the attack. } 1. e4 e6 2. d3 d5 { d5: Black claims central space and challenges White to justify the restrained d3 setup. } 3. Nd2 Nf6 4. Ngf3 Be7 { Be7: Black changes a bishop's diagonal; identify the pawn break that will make the diagonal useful. } 5. g3 O-O 6. Bg2 c5 { c5: Black attacks d4 and accelerates queenside expansion. } 7. O-O Nc6 8. Re1 b5 { b5: Black gains queenside space and begins the race against White's king attack. } 9. e5 Nd7 10. h4 b4 { b4: Black changes the pawn skeleton; reassess the chain, breaks, weak squares, and king exposure. } 11. Nf1 a5 { a5: Black changes the pawn skeleton; reassess the chain, breaks, weak squares, and king exposure. Black plan: Black occupies d4 when it cannot be chased, exchanges a key attacker, and coordinates queenside pressure with the central outpost. White plan: White controls d4 with c3 or reroutes pieces so an occupation can be exchanged without ruining the attack. Decision: Is d4 a harmless square, a blockading square, or an entry square that creates a concrete threat? Danger: Treating the closed center as completely safe while a black piece dominates d4. Memory rule: In the KIA, d4 is the square White concedes; monitor who can use it and why. } *

