{ Volume 3: The KIA Piece Routes. Why it matters: Nf1 clears d2, protects h2, and prepares h2-g4 or e3, but it temporarily reduces queenside influence and costs a tempo. } 1. e4 e6 2. d3 d5 { d5: Black claims central space and challenges White to justify the restrained d3 setup. } 3. Nd2 Nf6 4. Ngf3 Be7 { Be7: Black changes a bishop's diagonal; identify the pawn break that will make the diagonal useful. } 5. g3 O-O 6. Bg2 c5 { c5: Black attacks d4 and accelerates queenside expansion. } 7. O-O Nc6 8. Re1 b5 { b5: Black gains queenside space and begins the race against White's king attack. } 9. e5 Nd7 10. h4 a5 { a5: Black changes the pawn skeleton; reassess the chain, breaks, weak squares, and king exposure. } 11. Nf1 { Nf1: White reroutes a knight to f1; name the destination job and the useful move conceded to the opponent. Black plan: Black uses the maneuvering time for ...b4, ...a5-a4, or ...f6 and tries to make the knight's destination irrelevant. White plan: White routes the knight only when h2, g4, or e3 contributes to the actual attack or central defense. Decision: What exact square will the f1 knight occupy next, and what useful move can Black make during the journey? Danger: Playing Nf1 because it is thematic when e3, h2, and g3 are all poor destinations. Memory rule: A retreat is an investment; write the destination and job before paying the tempo. } *

