{ Volume 3: The KIA Piece Routes. Why it matters: The knight from f1 can reach h2 and g4, where it attacks f6 and h6 and supports a kingside pawn break without blocking the g-pawn. } 1. e4 e6 2. d3 d5 { d5: Black claims central space and challenges White to justify the restrained d3 setup. } 3. Nd2 Nf6 4. Ngf3 Be7 { Be7: Black changes a bishop's diagonal; identify the pawn break that will make the diagonal useful. } 5. g3 O-O 6. Bg2 c5 { c5: Black attacks d4 and accelerates queenside expansion. } 7. O-O Nc6 8. Re1 b5 { b5: Black gains queenside space and begins the race against White's king attack. } 9. e5 Nd7 10. h4 a5 { a5: Black changes the pawn skeleton; reassess the chain, breaks, weak squares, and king exposure. } 11. Nf1 b4 12. N1h2 a4 { a4: Black changes the pawn skeleton; reassess the chain, breaks, weak squares, and king exposure. } 13. Ng4 { Ng4: White reroutes a knight to g4; name the destination job and the useful move conceded to the opponent. Black plan: Black challenges the knight with ...h5 or ...f5, opens the center, or attacks d3 before the kingside pieces settle. White plan: White coordinates Ng4 with h5, Bf4, and queen entry squares so the knight's arrival creates a concrete threat. Decision: After Ng4, what target is attacked twice and what defensive pawn move can drive the knight away? Danger: Completing the long route when the g4 knight can be chased without creating a weakness. Memory rule: The h2-g4 route works when the knight's arrival forces a concession. } *

