{ Volume 3: The KIA Piece Routes. Why it matters: The g2 bishop may appear blocked by e4, but it protects the king, supports central dark squares, and can awaken after e5-e6, exd5, or a queenside transformation. } 1. Nf3 d5 2. g3 Nf6 { Nf6: Black reroutes a knight to f6; name the destination job and the useful move conceded to the opponent. } 3. Bg2 e6 4. O-O Be7 { Be7: Black changes a bishop's diagonal; identify the pawn break that will make the diagonal useful. } 5. d3 O-O 6. Nbd2 c5 { c5: Black attacks d4 and accelerates queenside expansion. } 7. e4 Nc6 8. Re1 b5 { b5: Black gains queenside space and begins the race against White's king attack. } 9. e5 Nd7 { Nd7: Black reroutes a knight to d7; name the destination job and the useful move conceded to the opponent. Black plan: Black keeps the diagonal closed, uses d4, and seeks queenside play while White's bishop has no direct target. White plan: White judges the bishop by its defensive and latent value, then opens the diagonal only when the center change helps the entire army. Decision: Which pawn move would activate the bishop, and would that move improve or expose White's center? Danger: Forcing the diagonal open merely to justify the bishop while conceding the stronger central structure. Memory rule: A fianchetto bishop can be valuable while hidden; open it when the position, not the ego, demands it. } *

