{ Volume 4: Building the Center Correctly. Why it matters: When Black builds ...d5 and ...c5, White's delayed e4 creates a closed center only if the resulting space and piece routes justify allowing ...d4. } 1. Nf3 d5 2. g3 c5 { c5: Black attacks d4 and accelerates queenside expansion. } 3. Bg2 Nc6 4. O-O e5 { e5: Black builds a symmetrical center that requires White to create a new pawn break. } 5. d3 Nf6 6. Nbd2 Be7 { Be7: Black changes a bishop's diagonal; identify the pawn break that will make the diagonal useful. } 7. e4 O-O 8. Re1 { Re1: White places a rook behind a pawn break or file expected to open. Black plan: Black may advance ...d4, gain space, and use the queenside while White is still organizing the kingside. White plan: White decides whether to accept the closed center, challenge with c3, or switch to c4 pressure before the space deficit becomes fixed. Decision: After e4, can Black play ...d4 with gain, and what square or break will White use against that chain? Danger: Completing the KIA center when Black's broad center can close under ideal conditions. Memory rule: Before e4, calculate Black's strongest pawn advance, not only its captures. } *

