{ Volume 4: Building the Center Correctly. Why it matters: When Black already occupies e5, White's own e4 creates a symmetrical tension rather than the familiar e5 space wedge. } 1. Nf3 d6 2. g3 e5 { e5: Black builds a symmetrical center that requires White to create a new pawn break. } 3. Bg2 Nf6 4. O-O Be7 { Be7: Black changes a bishop's diagonal; identify the pawn break that will make the diagonal useful. } 5. d3 O-O 6. Nbd2 Nc6 { Nc6: Black reroutes a knight to c6; name the destination job and the useful move conceded to the opponent. } 7. e4 Re8 8. c3 Bf8 { Bf8: Black changes a bishop's diagonal; identify the pawn break that will make the diagonal useful. Black plan: Black prepares ...d5, improves the pieces, and uses central equality to neutralize a slow kingside buildup. White plan: White uses c3, Re1, and a timed d4 or f4 to create an imbalance instead of copying the closed French plan. Decision: Which break, d4 or f4, changes the symmetry on terms favorable to White? Danger: Trying to play e5 into a well-supported black e5 pawn or maneuvering without creating tension. Memory rule: Against Black's ...e5, the KIA needs a break; the French attacking script no longer applies. } *

