{ Volume 4: Building the Center Correctly. Why it matters: Some black setups offer no useful e5 attack and allow White to claim a stronger classical center with d4 and c4. } 1. Nf3 d5 2. g3 Nf6 { Nf6: Black reroutes a knight to f6; name the destination job and the useful move conceded to the opponent. } 3. Bg2 e6 4. O-O Be7 { Be7: Black changes a bishop's diagonal; identify the pawn break that will make the diagonal useful. } 5. d4 O-O 6. c4 c6 { c6: Black changes the pawn skeleton; reassess the chain, breaks, weak squares, and king exposure. } 7. Qc2 Nbd7 { Nbd7: Black reroutes a knight to d7; name the destination job and the useful move conceded to the opponent. Black plan: Black builds a solid center, seeks ...b6 or ...dxc4, and challenges White to prove an advantage outside KIA patterns. White plan: White accepts a Catalan or Réti-type structure because the center, bishop, and space work better than an automatic d3-e4 shell. Decision: Does d3 preserve useful flexibility, or does d4 claim space without allowing Black an active break? Danger: Refusing a favorable center because it no longer carries the King's Indian Attack label. Memory rule: The KIA is a tool, not a vow; take the center when Black gives it safely. } *

