{ Volume 5: Architecture of the Kingside Attack. Why it matters: Re1 reinforces e4-e5, supports a later e5-e6 sacrifice, and gives the rook a role if the e-file opens. } 1. e4 e6 2. d3 d5 { d5: Black claims central space and challenges White to justify the restrained d3 setup. } 3. Nd2 Nf6 4. Ngf3 Be7 { Be7: Black changes a bishop's diagonal; identify the pawn break that will make the diagonal useful. } 5. g3 O-O 6. Bg2 c5 { c5: Black attacks d4 and accelerates queenside expansion. } 7. O-O Nc6 8. Re1 b5 { b5: Black gains queenside space and begins the race against White's king attack. } 9. e5 Nd7 { Nd7: Black reroutes a knight to d7; name the destination job and the useful move conceded to the opponent. Black plan: Black keeps the e-file closed, attacks the chain with ...f6, and uses queenside tempi before White's rook becomes active. White plan: White treats Re1 as preparation for a central rupture, not as proof that an attack already exists. Decision: What pawn exchange will open the e-file, and which king or piece will then stand on it? Danger: Placing the rook on e1 and never creating a condition in which the file can open. Memory rule: A rook belongs behind the pawn break that may open its file. } *

