{ Volume 5: Architecture of the Kingside Attack. Why it matters: The attack needs queen access through e2, h5, or g4 and a useful diagonal for the c1 bishop; otherwise the pawns advance without heavy-piece support. } 1. e4 e6 2. d3 d5 { d5: Black claims central space and challenges White to justify the restrained d3 setup. } 3. Nd2 Nf6 4. Ngf3 Be7 { Be7: Black changes a bishop's diagonal; identify the pawn break that will make the diagonal useful. } 5. g3 O-O 6. Bg2 c5 { c5: Black attacks d4 and accelerates queenside expansion. } 7. O-O Nc6 8. Re1 b5 { b5: Black gains queenside space and begins the race against White's king attack. } 9. e5 Nd7 10. h4 a5 { a5: Black changes the pawn skeleton; reassess the chain, breaks, weak squares, and king exposure. } 11. Nf1 b4 12. Bf4 { Bf4: White changes a bishop's diagonal; identify the pawn break that will make the diagonal useful. Black plan: Black controls entry squares, trades one attacking bishop, and opens the center while White's queen is traveling. White plan: White chooses Bf4, Qe2, and h5 in an order that avoids tempo attacks and creates a concrete threat. Decision: Which queen square is safe, and which bishop move makes that square tactically meaningful? Danger: Sending the queen to the kingside where a minor piece can attack it while Black gains central time. Memory rule: An attack becomes real when the queen has an entry square and the minor pieces control its escape squares. } *

