{ Volume 5: Architecture of the Kingside Attack. Why it matters: Kingside space is not an attack by itself. White needs enough pieces near the king to exploit an opened file before Black's defenders arrive. } 1. e4 e6 2. d3 d5 { d5: Black claims central space and challenges White to justify the restrained d3 setup. } 3. Nd2 Nf6 4. Ngf3 Be7 { Be7: Black changes a bishop's diagonal; identify the pawn break that will make the diagonal useful. } 5. g3 O-O 6. Bg2 c5 { c5: Black attacks d4 and accelerates queenside expansion. } 7. O-O Nc6 8. Re1 b5 { b5: Black gains queenside space and begins the race against White's king attack. } 9. e5 Nd7 10. h4 a5 { a5: Black changes the pawn skeleton; reassess the chain, breaks, weak squares, and king exposure. } 11. Nf1 b4 12. N1h2 a4 { a4: Black changes the pawn skeleton; reassess the chain, breaks, weak squares, and king exposure. } 13. Ng4 { Ng4: White reroutes a knight to g4; name the destination job and the useful move conceded to the opponent. Black plan: Black transfers a knight or rook to the kingside, exchanges a key attacker, or creates a central threat that forces White to retreat. White plan: White counts pieces that can join within one move and chooses a pawn contact only when the attackers outnumber useful defenders. Decision: After the next pawn exchange, how many white pieces attack the opened squares and how many black pieces defend them? Danger: Counting advanced pawns as attackers even when no piece can use the file they open. Memory rule: Open a line only when your pieces, not merely your pawns, can enter first. } *

