{ Volume 6: Timing e5, f4, and f5. Why it matters: e5 is strongest when the f6 knight must move to a less useful square and Black cannot immediately destroy the pawn chain with ...f6. } 1. e4 e6 2. d3 d5 { d5: Black claims central space and challenges White to justify the restrained d3 setup. } 3. Nd2 Nf6 4. Ngf3 Be7 { Be7: Black changes a bishop's diagonal; identify the pawn break that will make the diagonal useful. } 5. g3 O-O 6. Bg2 c5 { c5: Black attacks d4 and accelerates queenside expansion. } 7. O-O Nc6 8. Re1 b5 { b5: Black gains queenside space and begins the race against White's king attack. } 9. e5 Nd7 { Nd7: Black reroutes a knight to d7; name the destination job and the useful move conceded to the opponent. Black plan: Black chooses the best knight retreat, prepares ...f6, and uses the time White spent supporting e5 to expand elsewhere. White plan: White uses the knight tempo to play h4, Nf1, or Bf4 and establishes the attacking routes before the center is undermined. Decision: Where will the f6 knight go, and does its retreat help Black attack e5 or defend the king? Danger: Playing e5 when the knight retreat improves Black's position or when the pawn can be undermined immediately. Memory rule: e5 must win a useful tempo, not merely a visible square. } *

