{ Volume 6: Timing e5, f4, and f5. Why it matters: f5 makes contact with e6 and g6, can open the f-file or e-file, and may sacrifice a pawn to release White's pieces. } 1. e4 e6 2. d3 d5 { d5: Black claims central space and challenges White to justify the restrained d3 setup. } 3. Nd2 Nf6 4. Ngf3 Be7 { Be7: Black changes a bishop's diagonal; identify the pawn break that will make the diagonal useful. } 5. g3 O-O 6. Bg2 c5 { c5: Black attacks d4 and accelerates queenside expansion. } 7. O-O Nc6 8. Re1 b5 { b5: Black gains queenside space and begins the race against White's king attack. } 9. e5 Nd7 10. h4 a5 { a5: Black changes the pawn skeleton; reassess the chain, breaks, weak squares, and king exposure. } 11. Nf1 b4 12. Ng5 a4 { a4: Black changes the pawn skeleton; reassess the chain, breaks, weak squares, and king exposure. } 13. f4 b3 14. f5 exf5 { exf5: Black releases tension on f5; recalculate the pawn chain, open files, and the opponent's most forcing recapture. Black plan: Black chooses the capture that keeps lines closed, returns material if needed, and counterattacks d3 or the queenside. White plan: White calculates the recapture, rook activation, and queen entry before committing the f-pawn. Decision: After ...exf5, which white piece recaptures or uses the opened line, and is the e5 pawn still secure? Danger: Playing f5 because it is thematic while Black can capture and leave White with no active recapture. Memory rule: f5 is a line-opening operation; identify the piece that enters the line. } *

