[Variation "h4 creates the hook"]

{ Volume 7: The h-Pawn and g-Pawn Storm. Why it matters: h4 prepares h5 against ...h6 or a direct gain of space, while keeping the g-pawn available; it also loosens g3 and the white king. } 1. e4 e6 2. d3 d5 { d5: Black claims central space and challenges White to justify the restrained d3 setup. } 3. Nd2 Nf6 4. Ngf3 Be7 { Be7: Black changes a bishop's diagonal; identify the pawn break that will make the diagonal useful. } 5. g3 O-O 6. Bg2 c5 { c5: Black attacks d4 and accelerates queenside expansion. } 7. O-O Nc6 8. Re1 b5 { b5: Black gains queenside space and begins the race against White's king attack. } 9. e5 Nd7 10. h4 { h4: White creates a possible kingside hook and supports h5. Black plan: Black responds with ...f6, ...h6, or queenside speed and avoids creating a hook unless it serves a defensive purpose. White plan: White uses h4 to force a pawn decision, then opens the h-file only when a rook or queen can reach it. Decision: If Black ignores h4, what is White's next forcing move; if Black plays ...h6, which pawn becomes the target? Danger: Advancing h4-h5 while the h-file would open for Black's rook or White cannot bring a heavy piece. Memory rule: A pawn becomes a hook only when another pawn can make profitable contact with it. } *

