{ Volume 7: The h-Pawn and g-Pawn Storm. Why it matters: h5 restricts ...g6 and fixes h7, but it can also close the h-file and leave White needing a different entry route. } 1. e4 e6 2. d3 d5 { d5: Black claims central space and challenges White to justify the restrained d3 setup. } 3. Nd2 Nf6 4. Ngf3 Be7 { Be7: Black changes a bishop's diagonal; identify the pawn break that will make the diagonal useful. } 5. g3 O-O 6. Bg2 c5 { c5: Black attacks d4 and accelerates queenside expansion. } 7. O-O Nc6 8. Re1 b5 { b5: Black gains queenside space and begins the race against White's king attack. } 9. e5 Nd7 10. h4 a5 { a5: Black changes the pawn skeleton; reassess the chain, breaks, weak squares, and king exposure. } 11. h5 b4 { b4: Black changes the pawn skeleton; reassess the chain, breaks, weak squares, and king exposure. Black plan: Black keeps the structure closed, attacks d3, and uses ...f6 to challenge the center rather than weakening the h-pawn shell. White plan: White combines h5 with Nf1-h2-g4, Bf4, and queen access so the fixed pawn becomes a target. Decision: Did h5 create a target that White can attack, or did it lock the only file White could open? Danger: Confusing territorial gain with line access and discovering that every kingside file is closed. Memory rule: Fix a pawn only when your pieces can attack the fixed square. } *

