{ Volume 7: The h-Pawn and g-Pawn Storm. Why it matters: g4-g5 gains space and can drive a defender from f6, but it opens dark squares around White's own king and may leave the g-pawn unsupported. } 1. e4 e6 2. d3 d5 { d5: Black claims central space and challenges White to justify the restrained d3 setup. } 3. Nd2 Nf6 4. Ngf3 Be7 { Be7: Black changes a bishop's diagonal; identify the pawn break that will make the diagonal useful. } 5. g3 O-O 6. Bg2 c5 { c5: Black attacks d4 and accelerates queenside expansion. } 7. O-O Nc6 8. Re1 b5 { b5: Black gains queenside space and begins the race against White's king attack. } 9. e5 Nd7 10. h4 a5 { a5: Black changes the pawn skeleton; reassess the chain, breaks, weak squares, and king exposure. } 11. Nf1 b4 12. N1h2 a4 { a4: Black changes the pawn skeleton; reassess the chain, breaks, weak squares, and king exposure. } 13. g4 b3 14. g5 { g5: White changes the pawn skeleton; reassess the chain, breaks, weak squares, and king exposure. Black plan: Black opens the center or queenside, challenges g5 with ...f6, and uses the weakened f4 and h4 squares. White plan: White pushes only when the knight displacement or opened g-file produces a forcing continuation. Decision: Where does the targeted knight go, and does that retreat attack e5, g5, or White's king? Danger: Chasing a knight to a better defensive square while weakening the castled king. Memory rule: A pawn chase is good only when the chased piece loses function, not merely location. } *

