{ Volume 8: The Dark Bishop and Queen Battery. Why it matters: Bf4 develops the last minor piece, supports e5, and eyes d6 or h6, but the bishop can be attacked by ...f6 or ...g5. } 1. e4 e6 2. d3 d5 { d5: Black claims central space and challenges White to justify the restrained d3 setup. } 3. Nd2 Nf6 4. Ngf3 Be7 { Be7: Black changes a bishop's diagonal; identify the pawn break that will make the diagonal useful. } 5. g3 O-O 6. Bg2 c5 { c5: Black attacks d4 and accelerates queenside expansion. } 7. O-O Nc6 8. Re1 b5 { b5: Black gains queenside space and begins the race against White's king attack. } 9. e5 Nd7 10. h4 a5 { a5: Black changes the pawn skeleton; reassess the chain, breaks, weak squares, and king exposure. } 11. Nf1 b4 12. Bf4 { Bf4: White changes a bishop's diagonal; identify the pawn break that will make the diagonal useful. Black plan: Black challenges the bishop with a useful pawn move or uses the tempo to accelerate queenside play. White plan: White selects f4, g5, or h6 as a destination according to the black pawn structure and queen route. Decision: Which square will the bishop influence after Black's most forcing pawn challenge? Danger: Placing the bishop on f4 because it looks active when it can be chased without concession. Memory rule: Develop the bishop to support the next break, not to complete a diagram. } *

