{ Volume 8: The Dark Bishop and Queen Battery. Why it matters: Qe2 reinforces e5, connects the rooks, and can reach h5 or g4, while staying behind the e-pawn and away from many minor-piece attacks. } 1. e4 e6 2. d3 d5 { d5: Black claims central space and challenges White to justify the restrained d3 setup. } 3. Nd2 Nf6 4. Ngf3 Be7 { Be7: Black changes a bishop's diagonal; identify the pawn break that will make the diagonal useful. } 5. g3 O-O 6. Bg2 c5 { c5: Black attacks d4 and accelerates queenside expansion. } 7. O-O Nc6 8. Re1 b5 { b5: Black gains queenside space and begins the race against White's king attack. } 9. e5 Nd7 10. h4 a5 { a5: Black changes the pawn skeleton; reassess the chain, breaks, weak squares, and king exposure. } 11. Qe2 { Qe2: White coordinates center and wing plans, but must check whether the queen becomes a tempo target. Black plan: Black attacks e5 with ...f6, opens the center against the queen, or gains queenside time before it reaches the king. White plan: White uses Qe2 only when its dual central and attacking roles justify blocking the e-rook's line temporarily. Decision: Will Qe2 help after ...f6, and which queen route remains safe after the center opens? Danger: Moving the queen toward the attack while the e-file opens against it. Memory rule: A good queen move serves the center and the king attack at the same time. } *

