{ Volume 8: The Dark Bishop and Queen Battery. Why it matters: Against a kingside fianchetto, exchanging the g7 bishop can weaken dark squares, but the usual Bh6 route must fit the actual bishop placement and move order. } 1. Nf3 g6 2. g3 Bg7 { Bg7: Black changes a bishop's diagonal; identify the pawn break that will make the diagonal useful. } 3. Bg2 d6 4. O-O Nf6 { Nf6: Black reroutes a knight to f6; name the destination job and the useful move conceded to the opponent. } 5. d3 O-O 6. Nbd2 e5 { e5: Black builds a symmetrical center that requires White to create a new pawn break. } 7. e4 Nc6 8. c3 h6 { h6: Black changes the pawn skeleton; reassess the chain, breaks, weak squares, and king exposure. } 9. Re1 Be6 10. Bf1 Qd7 { Qd7: Black coordinates center and wing plans, but must check whether the queen becomes a tempo target. } 11. Qc2 { Qc2: White coordinates center and wing plans, but must check whether the queen becomes a tempo target. Black plan: Black preserves the g7 bishop, challenges the center, and uses ...Bh3 or ...d5 before White completes a slow battery. White plan: White coordinates the dark bishop and queen, but switches plans if the exchange costs too many tempi or closes useful diagonals. Decision: Does exchanging the fianchetto bishop create entry squares, or does it remove White's own useful attacking bishop? Danger: Spending several moves to trade a bishop while Black seizes the center and the remaining pieces lack targets. Memory rule: Exchange a king defender only when the squares it guarded can be occupied. } *

