{ Volume 9: The KIA against the French. Why it matters: The French KIA gives White a familiar kingside space wedge while Black receives a large queenside and the thematic ...f6 break. } 1. e4 e6 2. d3 d5 { d5: Black claims central space and challenges White to justify the restrained d3 setup. } 3. Nd2 Nf6 4. Ngf3 Be7 { Be7: Black changes a bishop's diagonal; identify the pawn break that will make the diagonal useful. } 5. g3 O-O 6. Bg2 c5 { c5: Black attacks d4 and accelerates queenside expansion. } 7. O-O Nc6 8. Re1 b5 { b5: Black gains queenside space and begins the race against White's king attack. } 9. e5 Nd7 10. h4 { h4: White creates a possible kingside hook and supports h5. Black plan: Black expands with ...b5-b4, attacks d3, and times ...f6 to destroy e5 before White's pieces surround the king. White plan: White completes Nf1-h2-g4, Bf4, and h5 while monitoring the speed and tactical effect of ...f6. Decision: Which side's next forcing pawn contact changes the position first: ...f6, ...b4, h5, or f5? Danger: Assuming the kingside attack is automatically faster because the black king is the visible target. Memory rule: The French KIA is a race; count pawn contacts and forcing replies, not board distance. } *

