{ Volume 9: The KIA against the French. Why it matters: Black's ...b5-b4 and ...a5-a4 gain space, attack c3 or d3, and may open files against White's queen before the kingside attack lands. } 1. e4 e6 2. d3 d5 { d5: Black claims central space and challenges White to justify the restrained d3 setup. } 3. Nd2 Nf6 4. Ngf3 Be7 { Be7: Black changes a bishop's diagonal; identify the pawn break that will make the diagonal useful. } 5. g3 O-O 6. Bg2 c5 { c5: Black attacks d4 and accelerates queenside expansion. } 7. O-O Nc6 8. Re1 b5 { b5: Black gains queenside space and begins the race against White's king attack. } 9. e5 Nd7 10. h4 a5 { a5: Black changes the pawn skeleton; reassess the chain, breaks, weak squares, and king exposure. } 11. Nf1 b4 12. N1h2 a4 { a4: Black changes the pawn skeleton; reassess the chain, breaks, weak squares, and king exposure. Black plan: Black uses each pawn move to gain a tempo or create a file and avoids overextension that gives White a central break. White plan: White decides whether to ignore, restrain with a3 or c3, or open the center so the queenside pawns become weaknesses. Decision: Does Black's next pawn advance create a threat White must answer, or can White spend that tempo on the king attack? Danger: Reacting to every queenside pawn move and never completing White's own forcing plan. Memory rule: Answer a wing move only when it changes the center, opens a file, or attacks something real. } *

