{ Volume 9: The KIA against the French. Why it matters: ...f6 attacks the head of White's chain and opens the f-file, e6 square, and diagonal structure; it is Black's main strategic equalizer. } 1. e4 e6 2. d3 d5 { d5: Black claims central space and challenges White to justify the restrained d3 setup. } 3. Nd2 Nf6 4. Ngf3 Be7 { Be7: Black changes a bishop's diagonal; identify the pawn break that will make the diagonal useful. } 5. g3 O-O 6. Bg2 c5 { c5: Black attacks d4 and accelerates queenside expansion. } 7. O-O Nc6 8. Re1 b5 { b5: Black gains queenside space and begins the race against White's king attack. } 9. e5 Nd7 10. h4 f6 { f6: Black strikes at the head of White's e5 pawn chain and opens tactical lines. } 11. exf6 Nxf6 { Nxf6: Black releases tension on f6; recalculate the pawn chain, open files, and the opponent's most forcing recapture. Black plan: Black prepares the break so the recapture develops a piece and the opened lines do not expose the king. White plan: White chooses exf6, a tactical e6 advance, or support of e5 based on which files and squares benefit the attack. Decision: After ...f6, which recapture activates White's pieces, and is the black king safer or more exposed? Danger: Keeping the e5 pawn at any cost while the rest of White's position becomes tied to its defense. Memory rule: When ...f6 arrives, evaluate the opened lines before deciding whether e5 must survive. } *

