{ Volume 10: The KIA against the Sicilian. Why it matters: Black's ...e6 setup controls d5 and prepares ...d5, while White keeps the center compact and seeks e5 or c3-d4 under favorable conditions. } 1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 e6 { e6: Black changes the pawn skeleton; reassess the chain, breaks, weak squares, and king exposure. } 3. d3 Nc6 4. g3 g6 { g6: Black changes the pawn skeleton; reassess the chain, breaks, weak squares, and king exposure. } 5. Bg2 Bg7 6. O-O Nge7 { Nge7: Black reroutes a knight to e7; name the destination job and the useful move conceded to the opponent. } 7. Nbd2 O-O 8. Re1 d6 { d6: Black changes the pawn skeleton; reassess the chain, breaks, weak squares, and king exposure. Black plan: Black achieves ...d5, uses the queenside majority, and prevents White from building a free kingside attack. White plan: White chooses e5 only if it gains useful space; otherwise c3-d4 or h4-f4 can create the needed imbalance. Decision: Can Black play ...d5 in one move, and does White's next move restrain or exploit that break? Danger: Playing a French-style e5 plan when Black's pieces and pawns control the square more efficiently. Memory rule: Against ...e6 Sicilians, the ...d5 break is the first question. } *

