{ Volume 10: The KIA against the Sicilian. Why it matters: The ...d6 move order slows ...d5 but supports ...e5 and a Dragon-like fianchetto; White has more time but fewer direct targets. } 1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 { d6: Black changes the pawn skeleton; reassess the chain, breaks, weak squares, and king exposure. } 3. d3 Nc6 4. g3 g6 { g6: Black changes the pawn skeleton; reassess the chain, breaks, weak squares, and king exposure. } 5. Bg2 Bg7 6. O-O e6 { e6: Black changes the pawn skeleton; reassess the chain, breaks, weak squares, and king exposure. } 7. Nbd2 Nge7 8. Re1 O-O { O-O: Black secures the king and activates a rook; the center can now close or open with less tactical risk. Black plan: Black expands with ...b5, prepares ...d5 or ...e5, and uses the c-file if White eventually plays d4. White plan: White improves pieces, selects c3-d4 or h4-f4, and avoids an attack whose pawn contacts never reach the king. Decision: Which central break will Black achieve first, and what white setup makes that break least comfortable? Danger: Launching h4-h5 into a closed fianchetto without central support or additional attackers. Memory rule: Against ...d6, use the extra time to choose a center, not merely to push farther on the wing. } *

