{ Volume 13: KIA Structures against ...e5, Caro-Kann, and Philidor. Why it matters: Against ...e5, White reaches a reversed closed king-pawn structure where d4 and f4, not e5, are the critical breaks. } 1. Nf3 Nc6 2. g3 e5 { e5: Black builds a symmetrical center that requires White to create a new pawn break. } 3. Bg2 Nf6 4. O-O Be7 { Be7: Black changes a bishop's diagonal; identify the pawn break that will make the diagonal useful. } 5. d3 O-O 6. Nbd2 d6 { d6: Black changes the pawn skeleton; reassess the chain, breaks, weak squares, and king exposure. } 7. e4 Re8 { Re8: Black places a rook behind a pawn break or file expected to open. Black plan: Black prepares ...Bf8, ...d5, or queenside activity and uses the equal center to neutralize slow maneuvers. White plan: White uses Re1, c3, and a timed d4 or f4 to exploit the extra tempo without overextending. Decision: Which break opens a useful line for the g2 bishop and preserves king safety? Danger: Calling the position a KIA and forcing an e5 advance into Black's occupied square. Memory rule: Against ...e5, play the position as a reversed king-pawn opening and create a break. } *

