{ Volume 13: KIA Structures against ...e5, Caro-Kann, and Philidor. Why it matters: Black's d6-e5 shell is solid but slightly cramped; White can build slowly, yet must decide whether d4 or c3-d4 is the true source of advantage. } 1. e4 d6 2. d3 Nf6 { Nf6: Black reroutes a knight to f6; name the destination job and the useful move conceded to the opponent. } 3. Nd2 e5 4. Ngf3 Be7 { Be7: Black changes a bishop's diagonal; identify the pawn break that will make the diagonal useful. } 5. g3 O-O 6. Bg2 Nbd7 { Nbd7: Black reroutes a knight to d7; name the destination job and the useful move conceded to the opponent. } 7. O-O c6 8. Re1 Qc7 { Qc7: Black coordinates center and wing plans, but must check whether the queen becomes a tempo target. Black plan: Black seeks ...Re8, ...Bf8, and ...d5, using the restrained structure to remove White's first-move pressure. White plan: White improves pieces, controls d4, and opens the center when the g2 bishop and rooks benefit. Decision: Can White play d4 directly, and what black recapture or break follows? Danger: Maneuvering indefinitely because the structure looks familiar while Black achieves ...d5 for free. Memory rule: A compact black center should be challenged before it completes its freeing break. } *

