{ Volume 14: Surviving Black's Central Breaks. Why it matters: ...c5 attacks d4, creates queenside space, and supports ...b5; it does not directly attack e5 but accelerates the wing race. } 1. e4 e6 2. d3 d5 { d5: Black claims central space and challenges White to justify the restrained d3 setup. } 3. Nd2 Nf6 4. Ngf3 Be7 { Be7: Black changes a bishop's diagonal; identify the pawn break that will make the diagonal useful. } 5. g3 O-O 6. Bg2 c5 { c5: Black attacks d4 and accelerates queenside expansion. } 7. O-O Nc6 8. Re1 b5 { b5: Black gains queenside space and begins the race against White's king attack. } 9. e5 Nd7 { Nd7: Black reroutes a knight to d7; name the destination job and the useful move conceded to the opponent. Black plan: Black gains space with ...b5-b4 and creates pressure on d3 while White's pieces move toward the kingside. White plan: White ignores harmless space, restrains real breakthroughs, and accelerates the kingside operation when Black spends non-forcing tempi. Decision: Does ...c5 create an immediate threat, or does it give White one more move for the attack? Danger: Reacting to space itself and losing the race by answering moves that threaten nothing. Memory rule: Distinguish a space move from a forcing move; answer only what changes the position. } *

