{ Volume 14: Surviving Black's Central Breaks. Why it matters: In symmetrical ...e5 structures, ...d5 frees Black's pieces and challenges White's center; allowing it under ideal conditions can erase the opening advantage. } 1. Nf3 d6 2. g3 e5 { e5: Black builds a symmetrical center that requires White to create a new pawn break. } 3. Bg2 Nf6 4. O-O Be7 { Be7: Black changes a bishop's diagonal; identify the pawn break that will make the diagonal useful. } 5. d3 O-O 6. Nbd2 Nc6 { Nc6: Black reroutes a knight to c6; name the destination job and the useful move conceded to the opponent. } 7. e4 Re8 8. c3 Bf8 { Bf8: Black changes a bishop's diagonal; identify the pawn break that will make the diagonal useful. } 9. Qc2 d5 { d5: Black claims central space and challenges White to justify the restrained d3 setup. Black plan: Black prepares the break with pieces and chooses exchanges that activate bishops and rooks. White plan: White restrains ...d5, captures only when the open files favor White, or plays d4 first to control the transformation. Decision: After ...d5, which recapture opens a file for whose rook, and which central pawn becomes isolated? Danger: Treating every central exchange as simplification without comparing the resulting piece activity. Memory rule: A freeing break is judged by the pieces it releases, not by the pawn it trades. } *

