{ Volume 14: Surviving Black's Central Breaks. Why it matters: ...f6 directly challenges the e5 wedge and can open the f-file toward both kings, turning a strategic attack into concrete calculation. } 1. e4 e6 2. d3 d5 { d5: Black claims central space and challenges White to justify the restrained d3 setup. } 3. Nd2 Nf6 4. Ngf3 Be7 { Be7: Black changes a bishop's diagonal; identify the pawn break that will make the diagonal useful. } 5. g3 O-O 6. Bg2 c5 { c5: Black attacks d4 and accelerates queenside expansion. } 7. O-O Nc6 8. Re1 b5 { b5: Black gains queenside space and begins the race against White's king attack. } 9. e5 Nd7 10. h4 f6 { f6: Black strikes at the head of White's e5 pawn chain and opens tactical lines. Black plan: Black times the break when the king has enough defenders and the recapture develops a piece or gains central control. White plan: White evaluates exf6, e6, or support of e5 by checking every opened diagonal and the e-file battery. Decision: Which side benefits from the f-file and e-file after each possible pawn exchange? Danger: Protecting the e5 pawn reflexively and missing a stronger line-opening sacrifice. Memory rule: Against ...f6, preserve the attack, not necessarily the pawn. } *

