{ Volume 15: When to Change the Structure. Why it matters: c3-d4 challenges Black's e5 center and can release the g2 bishop, turning slow maneuvering into an open central game. } 1. Nf3 d6 2. g3 e5 { e5: Black builds a symmetrical center that requires White to create a new pawn break. } 3. Bg2 Nf6 4. O-O Be7 { Be7: Black changes a bishop's diagonal; identify the pawn break that will make the diagonal useful. } 5. d3 O-O 6. Nbd2 Nc6 { Nc6: Black reroutes a knight to c6; name the destination job and the useful move conceded to the opponent. } 7. e4 Re8 8. c3 Bf8 { Bf8: Black changes a bishop's diagonal; identify the pawn break that will make the diagonal useful. } 9. Qc2 a5 10. d4 { d4: White changes the KIA shell into an open central fight. Black plan: Black maintains tension, exchanges on d4, or plays ...d5 if the resulting activity equalizes. White plan: White prepares d4 until the recapture develops a piece or opens the e-file on favorable terms. Decision: After d4, which pawn recapture keeps White's center coherent and which black piece gains a square? Danger: Playing d4 merely because preparation is complete while the resulting exchanges favor Black. Memory rule: Prepare a break by improving the position after every likely recapture. } *

