{ Volume 15: When to Change the Structure. Why it matters: c4 attacks d5 and may create a Réti or reversed Benoni structure where the g2 bishop and queenside pressure matter more than a kingside pawn storm. } 1. Nf3 d5 2. g3 Nf6 { Nf6: Black reroutes a knight to f6; name the destination job and the useful move conceded to the opponent. } 3. Bg2 e6 4. O-O Be7 { Be7: Black changes a bishop's diagonal; identify the pawn break that will make the diagonal useful. } 5. d3 O-O 6. c4 c5 { c5: Black attacks d4 and accelerates queenside expansion. } 7. Nc3 d4 8. Nb1 Nc6 { Nc6: Black reroutes a knight to c6; name the destination job and the useful move conceded to the opponent. Black plan: Black gains space with ...d4 and uses the center to restrict White's knights. White plan: White undermines the chain with e3, b4, or e4 and reroutes the displaced knight toward useful squares. Decision: If Black plays ...d4, what break attacks the chain's base and where will the b1 knight develop? Danger: Switching to c4 without accepting the strategic work required after ...d4. Memory rule: A structural switch is good only when you know the opponent's most ambitious reply. } *

