{ Volume 15: When to Change the Structure. Why it matters: When Black exchanges on e4, White's d-pawn recapture opens the d-file and frees the c1 bishop, but may leave an isolated or targetable e-pawn. } 1. Nf3 d5 2. g3 Nf6 { Nf6: Black reroutes a knight to f6; name the destination job and the useful move conceded to the opponent. } 3. Bg2 e6 4. O-O Be7 { Be7: Black changes a bishop's diagonal; identify the pawn break that will make the diagonal useful. } 5. d3 O-O 6. Nbd2 c5 { c5: Black attacks d4 and accelerates queenside expansion. } 7. e4 Nc6 8. Re1 dxe4 { dxe4: Black releases tension on e4; recalculate the pawn chain, open files, and the opponent's most forcing recapture. } 9. dxe4 { dxe4: White releases tension on e4; recalculate the pawn chain, open files, and the opponent's most forcing recapture. Black plan: Black exchanges pieces, pressures e4, and uses the d-file before White completes coordination. White plan: White uses the open lines and development gain, accepting a different middlegame instead of rebuilding the closed KIA shell. Decision: Which recapture activates more pieces, and what permanent pawn target does it create? Danger: Recapturing toward the familiar pawn shape while choosing the less active piece placement. Memory rule: When the center changes, keep the active recapture, not the old opening picture. } *

