{ Volume 16: KIA Tactical Grammar. Why it matters: After f5 draws the e6 pawn away, e5-e6 can sacrifice the advanced pawn to open files, dislodge f7, and give the rook or queen entry squares against the king. } 1. e4 e6 2. d3 d5 { d5: Black claims central space and challenges White to justify the restrained d3 setup. } 3. Nd2 Nf6 4. Ngf3 Be7 { Be7: Black changes a bishop's diagonal; identify the pawn break that will make the diagonal useful. } 5. g3 O-O 6. Bg2 c5 { c5: Black attacks d4 and accelerates queenside expansion. } 7. O-O Nc6 8. Re1 b5 { b5: Black gains queenside space and begins the race against White's king attack. } 9. e5 Nd7 10. h4 a5 { a5: Black changes the pawn skeleton; reassess the chain, breaks, weak squares, and king exposure. } 11. Nf1 b4 12. Ng5 a4 { a4: Black changes the pawn skeleton; reassess the chain, breaks, weak squares, and king exposure. } 13. f4 b3 14. f5 exf5 { exf5: Black releases tension on f5; recalculate the pawn chain, open files, and the opponent's most forcing recapture. } 15. e6 Nf6 16. exf7+ Rxf7 { Rxf7: Black releases tension on f7; recalculate the pawn chain, open files, and the opponent's most forcing recapture. Black plan: Black calculates whether accepting leaves enough defenders, declines when the closed position is safer, and returns material to trade attackers. White plan: White plays e6 only when a rook, queen, or bishop can use the opened line immediately and the return sacrifice remains forcing. Decision: After ...fxe6, what is White's first forcing move and can Black exchange the invading piece? Danger: Sacrificing the e-pawn because the motif is famous when the e-file has no attacker. Memory rule: A clearance sacrifice needs an open line, an entering piece, and a forcing second move. } *

