{ Volume 18: A Lifetime King's Indian Attack System. Why it matters: The classical rehearsal links e5 space, Re1, h4, Nf1-h2-g4, and Bf4 or Qe2 into one plan while Black races with ...f6 and queenside expansion. } 1. e4 e6 2. d3 d5 { d5: Black claims central space and challenges White to justify the restrained d3 setup. } 3. Nd2 Nf6 4. Ngf3 Be7 { Be7: Black changes a bishop's diagonal; identify the pawn break that will make the diagonal useful. } 5. g3 O-O 6. Bg2 c5 { c5: Black attacks d4 and accelerates queenside expansion. } 7. O-O Nc6 8. Re1 b5 { b5: Black gains queenside space and begins the race against White's king attack. } 9. e5 Nd7 10. h4 a5 { a5: Black changes the pawn skeleton; reassess the chain, breaks, weak squares, and king exposure. } 11. Nf1 b4 12. N1h2 a4 { a4: Black changes the pawn skeleton; reassess the chain, breaks, weak squares, and king exposure. } 13. Ng4 { Ng4: White reroutes a knight to g4; name the destination job and the useful move conceded to the opponent. Black plan: Black identifies the slowest part of White's buildup and strikes in the center or on the queenside before every attacker arrives. White plan: White preserves the causal order: secure the center, route pieces, create a hook, then open a line with enough force. Decision: Which move is essential to the position, and which familiar move can be omitted because Black changed the threat? Danger: Reproducing the full setup when one black move has made the usual route too slow or tactically wrong. Memory rule: Store the causal chain, not the diagram: center, route, hook, contact, entry. } *

