{ Volume 18: A Lifetime King's Indian Attack System. Why it matters: A lifetime KIA repertoire begins by classifying Black's center: French ...e6-d5, Sicilian ...c5, symmetrical ...e5, fianchetto ...g6, or flexible ...b6. } 1. Nf3 d5 2. g3 Nf6 { Nf6: Black reroutes a knight to f6; name the destination job and the useful move conceded to the opponent. } 3. Bg2 e6 4. O-O Be7 { Be7: Black changes a bishop's diagonal; identify the pawn break that will make the diagonal useful. } 5. d3 O-O 6. Nbd2 c5 { c5: Black attacks d4 and accelerates queenside expansion. } 7. e4 Nc6 8. Re1 { Re1: White places a rook behind a pawn break or file expected to open. Black plan: Black aims for the freeing break appropriate to the structure and punishes White for using the wrong stock maneuver. White plan: White selects the matching center and route, accepting d4 or c4 structures when they are objectively more active. Decision: Which black break is easiest, and what white move changes its tactical or strategic value? Danger: Naming the opening before identifying the pawn structure and therefore importing the wrong plan. Memory rule: Classify the center first; the opening name is only an index. } *

