{ Volume 18: A Lifetime King's Indian Attack System. Why it matters: Because the KIA can arise against many defenses, efficient study repairs the first decision you could not explain instead of adding unrelated branches. } 1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 e6 { e6: Black changes the pawn skeleton; reassess the chain, breaks, weak squares, and king exposure. } 3. d3 Nc6 4. g3 g6 { g6: Black changes the pawn skeleton; reassess the chain, breaks, weak squares, and king exposure. } 5. Bg2 Bg7 6. O-O Nge7 { Nge7: Black reroutes a knight to e7; name the destination job and the useful move conceded to the opponent. } 7. Nbd2 O-O 8. Re1 d6 { d6: Black changes the pawn skeleton; reassess the chain, breaks, weak squares, and king exposure. Black plan: Opponents vary move orders to achieve ...d5, ...e5, or queenside expansion before White recognizes the structure. White plan: White records the structure, candidate breaks, and strongest reply, then adds one corrected line and one verbal trigger to the file. Decision: At what move did the plan stop fitting the center, and what cue should trigger the better branch next time? Danger: Blaming the system after one loss or collecting hundreds of moves without practicing the decision that failed. Memory rule: Repair the first unexplained decision, rehearse it twice, and test it in a slow game. } *

